        "patch_notes": "Root cause: StringLookupFactory.addDefaultStringLookups(Map) unconditionally registered every DefaultStringLookup enum value — including SCRIPT (arbitrary JS/script engine execution via ScriptStringLookup), DNS, and URL (SSRF) — into the map used by the zero-arg StringSubstitutor.createInterpolator() and StringLookupFactory.interpolatorStringLookup(). Since createInterpolator() is the documented/typical way to apply interpolation to arbitrary strings, any caller passing attacker-influenced text through it exposed remote code execution via \"${script:javascript:...}\", matching all four traces in the alert (they differ only in which StringSubstitutor.replace overload/source buffer is used, but converge on the same ScriptStringLookup.evaluate sink).\n\nFix: in StringLookupFactory.addDefaultStringLookups(), skip DefaultStringLookup.SCRIPT, DefaultStringLookup.DNS, and DefaultStringLookup.URL while iterating DefaultStringLookup.values(), so they are no longer added to the default lookup map. All other default lookups (base64, const, date, env, file, java, localhost, properties, resourceBundle, sys, urlDecoder, urlEncoder, xml) are unaffected. Applications that intentionally need script/dns/url interpolation can still opt in explicitly via StringLookupFactory.interpolatorStringLookup(Map, StringLookup, boolean), passing a map that includes DefaultStringLookup.SCRIPT/DNS/URL — this path is exercised by updated tests.\n\nThis is the same remediation Apache Commons Text shipped for CVE-2022-42889 (Text4Shell) in 1.10.0. It's a single choke-point fix (not a per-call-site patch), so it blocks the vulnerable behavior across every current and future StringSubstitutor.createInterpolator()/interpolatorStringLookup() call site and every variable-name encoding that reaches it (StrBuilder/TextStringBuilder replace, StringBuilder replace, midString/varName paths, etc.), since they all funnel through the same default-lookup map.\n\nTest/doc updates:\n- StringLookupFactoryTest.assertDefaultKeys(): now asserts KEY_SCRIPT/KEY_DNS/KEY_URL are absent from the default map (previously asserted present).\n- StringSubstitutorWithInterpolatorStringLookupTest: added testDefaultInterpolatorDoesNotResolveDangerousLookups() as a direct regression test proving ${script:...}, ${dns:...}, ${url:...} pass through createInterpolator() unresolved. testDefaultInterpolator()'s doc-example string no longer includes the now-unsupported-by-default DNS/Script/URL lines. testDnsLookup*/testJavaScript were updated to build an interpolator via the explicit opt-in API (StringLookupFactory.interpolatorStringLookup(Map, null, true) with the specific DefaultStringLookup added) instead of relying on createInterpolator()'s default set, preserving coverage of those lookups' behavior.\n- Javadoc in StringSubstitutor.java and StringLookupFactory.java updated to document the exclusion and the opt-in mechanism.",
